      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "CONTEXT",
          "content": "Leptin has nitric oxide (NO)-dependent vasodilator actions, but hyperleptinemia is an independent risk factor for cardiovascular disease."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The objective of the study was to investigate whether, in the human circulation, properties of leptin to release NO are opposed by stimulation of vasculotoxic substances, such as endothelin (ET)-1, and whether this mechanism might contribute to vascular damage in hyperleptinemic states like obesity."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Forearm blood flow responses (plethysmography) to ETA receptor antagonism (BQ-123, 10 nmol/min) and NO synthase inhibition [N(G)-monomethyl L-arginine (L-NMMA), 4 μmol/min] were assessed before and after intraarterial administration of leptin (2 μg/min) in lean controls (n = 8) and patients with obesity-related metabolic syndrome (MetS; n = 8)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Baseline plasma leptin was higher in patients than in controls (P < .001). Before infusion of leptin, the vasodilator response to BQ-123 was greater in patients than in controls (P < .001), whereas infusion of L-NMMA induced higher vasoconstriction in controls than in patients (P = .04). In lean subjects, hyperleptinemia resulted in increased vasodilator response to ETA receptor antagonism (P < .001 vs before) and enhanced vasoconstrictor effect of L-NMMA during ETA receptor blockade (P = .015 vs before). In patients with the MetS, by contrast, vascular responses to both BQ-123 and L-NMMA were not modified by exogenous leptin (both P > .05 vs before)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "These findings indicate that, under physiological conditions, leptin stimulates both ET-1 and NO activity in the human circulation. This effect is absent in hyperleptinemic patients with the MetS who are unresponsive to additional leptin. In these patients, therefore, hyperleptinemia may be a biomarker of vascular dysfunction, rather than a mediator of vascular damage."
        }
      ]},
